Subject: give the guest its own loopback address with --net-ip

ThothDock user-defined networks (ThothDock docs/nextgen/adr/ADR-0002) give
each container one address in 127.77.0.0/16. With --net-ip=ADDR, PRoot
rewrites socket addresses at syscall entry:

- bind to 0.0.0.0, 127.0.0.0/8, :: or ::1 binds ADDR instead (dual-stack
  IPv6 sockets get the v4-mapped ADDR; a V6ONLY wildcard becomes an inert
  [::1]:0, so "listen [::]:80 ipv6only=on" servers still start);
- connect/sendto to loopback or 0.0.0.0 reaches ADDR, so localhost is the
  container's own; 127.77.0.1 reaches the host's 127.0.0.1;
- ports 1-1023 on 127.77.0.0/16 become port + 30000, because an
  unprivileged Android app cannot bind them.

The rewritten sockaddr is pushed on the tracee's stack (alloc_mem), never
written into the application's buffer. bind, connect and sendto are already
stopped on by PRoot's own seccomp filter, so no new ptrace stop is added.
Without --net-ip nothing changes.

